   "n": 16,
   "title": "Curia Julia, the Senate House",
   "core": "This tall brick building is the Senate House. Julius Caesar began it, Augustus completed it in 29 BC, and what stands now is Emperor Diocletian's rebuild after a fire in 283 AD. It survived because it became a church in the 7th century, and was restored to its Roman form in the 1930s. Its original bronze doors were moved in the 1600s to the Basilica of St John Lateran, where they still hang.\n\nHow did the Senate work? Senators were former magistrates, around 600 of them under Augustus. Officially they only gave advice. In practice, under the Republic, they controlled money, foreign policy and the provinces. They sat on tiered benches along the side walls. The presiding magistrate asked each senator for his opinion in order of rank. Then they voted by walking to one side of the hall, \"going with their feet\".\n\nUnder the emperors, real power moved to the palace. The Senate became the stage where the aristocracy showed loyalty, but it remained the top of every Roman career.",
   "deep": "After Actium, Augustus placed a statue and altar of the goddess Victory in this hall. For nearly four centuries, senators burned incense there before meetings. Christian emperors removed it, and in 382 AD Gratian removed it for good.\n\nIn 384 the pagan senator Symmachus, prefect of Rome, asked the young emperor Valentinian II to restore it. His most famous argument: so great a mystery cannot be reached by one road alone. It was a plea for tolerance and for the traditions that had made Rome.\n\nBishop Ambrose of Milan answered that truth is one, and a Christian emperor must not support idolatry. Ambrose won, and the altar never returned. The debate captures a turning point: an old world of many paths to the divine, and a new one built on a single truth.",
   "isCore": true,
   "deepTitle": "the Altar of Victory",
   "threads": [
    "Politics",
    "Philosophy"
   ],
   "note": "",
   "lookFor": "The brick facade, once covered in stucco made to look like marble. The bronze doors, today modern copies. If the interior is open: the tiers where the senators' benches stood.",
   "imagesPlanned": "Then and now: a reconstruction drawing of a Senate session. Photo: the original bronze doors at St John Lateran. Diagram to draw: how a vote by walking worked.",
   "section": 1,
