   "n": 17,
   "title": "Shrine of Venus Cloacina and Basilica Aemilia",
   "core": "In front of the Basilica Aemilia is a small round marble base. It was the shrine of Venus Cloacina, goddess of the Cloaca Maxima, the great drain. Tradition says the drain was begun around 600 BC, under Rome's kings, as an open channel carrying the valley's streams to the Tiber. Later it was vaulted over. Parts of it still carry water today, and its outlet can be seen on the Tiber bank. The Romans gave their sewer a goddess.\n\nPliny the Elder ranked the sewers among Rome's greatest achievements, and claimed they were big enough for a wagon loaded with hay to pass through. Water overflowing from the aqueducts kept them flushed.\n\nBehind the shrine, the Basilica Aemilia was a business hall lined with shops, first built in 179 BC. When the Visigoths sacked Rome in 410 AD it burned. Bronze coins in the money changers' shops melted into the marble floor, and you can still see the green stains.",
