   "n": 19,
   "title": "Outside: Nero's lake becomes a people's arena",
   "core": "Its real name is the Flavian Amphitheatre. Colosseum is a medieval nickname, probably from the Colossus of Nero, a bronze statue over 30 m tall that stood nearby.\n\nAfter the great fire of 64 AD, Nero built himself a vast palace and park in the centre of Rome, with an artificial lake on this spot. After his death in 68 and a year of civil war, the winner, Vespasian, drained the lake and built an arena for the public. The message: what the tyrant took, the new emperor gives back to the people. The poet Martial celebrated exactly that.\n\nWork began around 70 AD. Titus opened it in 80 AD with a hundred days of games, and Domitian completed it. Remember the treasure on the Arch of Titus this morning: the spoils of Jerusalem probably helped pay for it.",
   "deep": "First the lake was drained and a ring of concrete foundations poured, around 12 m deep. The outer wall used about 100,000 cubic metres of travertine, quarried near Tivoli about 30 km away and hauled on a dedicated road. The blocks were held together with an estimated 300 tonnes of iron clamps.\n\nThe speed came from organisation. Small differences in details between sections suggest that four building teams worked on four quarters of the ring at the same time. Parts were standardised. Treadwheel cranes, powered by men walking inside a large wheel, lifted blocks weighing several tonnes. The labour force combined skilled builders with large numbers of unskilled workers, probably including prisoners from the Jewish war.",
   "isCore": true,
   "deepTitle": "built in about ten years",
   "threads": [
    "Politics",
    "Engineering"
   ],
   "note": "",
   "lookFor": "The full four-storey outer wall on the north side, and the lower, broken south side. The two big sloping brick buttresses at the ends of the surviving outer wall, added in the early 1800s to stop it collapsing.",
   "imagesPlanned": "Then and now: a reconstruction of the valley with Nero's lake and the Colossus. Diagram to draw: a Roman treadwheel crane lifting a block. Photo: the 1800s brick buttress.",
   "section": 2,
